  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Using a prospective cohort of 98,560 women in late pregnancy in Nepal, this study evaluated the risk of pre-eclampsia following high ambient temperature exposure over a seven-day lag period. Exposure to extreme heat at or above the 95th percentile increased the cumulative odds of pre-eclampsia by 42% overall, with risk peaking on lag-day 3. Socially disadvantaged ethnic groups experienced a significant 61% increase in pre-eclampsia odds from heat exposure, compared to a non-significant 3% increase among advantaged groups.",
      "why": "The findings provide population-level evidence connecting acute heat exposure to late-gestation pre-eclampsia in a low-resource South Asian setting, demonstrating that social disadvantage substantially amplifies thermal health risks during pregnancy."
    }
  },
